blocks.explanation:
CRUD stands for Create, Read, Update and Delete; it defines the basic database actions.

1. Create: adds new data (a new record) to a table using the SQL INSERT command. Example: INSERT INTO students (ID, Name, Age) VALUES (2, 'Amna', 17);

2. Read: retrieves and displays data using the SQL SELECT command; it does not change the stored data. Example: SELECT * FROM students;

3. Update: modifies existing records using the SQL UPDATE command with a condition, used when information needs correction. Example: UPDATE students SET Age = 18 WHERE ID = 2;

4. Delete: removes unwanted records using the SQL DELETE command with a condition; careful use prevents loss of important data. Example: DELETE FROM students WHERE ID = 2;

Why: these operations manage the data stored in a database and keep the information accurate and up to date, so every database-based application depends on them. Python performs them by sending SQL commands.
